File Index

/autopilot-signals.ts
Relation: used by hooks/useAutopilotSignals.ts, which useAutoLeveling calls; the Copilot command gate (features/copilot/gate.ts) is the consumer. Change the event names together with features/copilot/autopilotLink.ts.

/autoleveling-actions.ts
Purpose: Parses and serializes autoleveling action scripts to/from the one-line-per-action editor format. Action kinds incl. send, send_cooldown (`cooldown <sec> <cmd>`), send_every_ticks (`every_ticks <n> <cmd>` — recast every N GMCP ticks, for affect-less buffs), wait_ms/text/regex/fighting, if_hp/mp/mv_below, if_affect_missing.

/autoleveling-alignment.ts
Purpose: alignmentXpModifier(player, target) — the DSL-customized XP rule (opposite alignment 2×, same 0.5×, any-neutral / unknown 1×). The engine divides observed tnl-drops by it to get a base kill-XP figure, then re-applies it for the "near a level-up" prediction. Alignment types live in autoleveling-types.ts (AutoLevelAlignment); the wizard captures them (not in GMCP).

/autoleveling-buff-catalog.ts
Purpose: BUFF_CATALOG — log-verified command↔GMCP-affect-name pairs for the common leveling buffs (sanc→sanctuary, stone→stone skin, fren→frenzy, 'detect inv'→detect invis, …). Consumed by CombatStep's "Add from catalog". Entries carry suggestInCombat (sanctuary is item-only in combat) / suggestHoldNearLevel (haste hurts mana regen); Berserk has affect:'' (no GMCP affect → tick gate).

/autoleveling-content-types.ts
Purpose: DTOs for the C#-served content — AutoPilotAreaSummary (index row), AutoPilotArea (detail: route dirs, level range, start room/speedwalk, recommendedTargets), AutoPilotTarget, AND the class catalog: AutoPilotClass ({name, mortalClass, isReclass, classGroup, abilities}) + AutoPilotAbility ({name, type skill|spell|song, level, groups[] — semantic IAbilityGroup names since IAbility.Usage/Duration/Group are unpopulated, helpFile?}). Mirrors Server.Dsl/Models/Autopilot.cs. normalizeAreaSummary / normalizeArea / normalizeClass / normalizeAbility COERCE rather than type-guard (C# serializer omits false/0/""). Buffs / fight scripts are NOT here — wizard-time client overlay in autoleveling-user-data.ts.

/autoleveling-classes.ts
Purpose: Read-through IndexedDB cache (shatteredarchive-maps DB, key autolevelingClasses, 7d soft TTL, network→cache fallback) of GET /api/web/maps/autoleveling/classes — getClassCatalog({force?}) + getClassCatalogCached(). Pure helpers: classByName (case-insensitive), classNames (base classes — !isReclass — first, each half A→Z), abilitiesForClass(list, name, {atOrBelowLevel?, type?, groups?}) (groups = any-of; no filter = every ability the class has — CombatStep's buff picker uses this directly now, see below). classOffensiveAbilities (OFFENSIVE_GROUPS ∩ groups, or a known COMBAT_SKILL_NAMES skill) is the one remaining group-heuristic helper, used only by the fight-command picker. __setClassStoreForTests seam. Mirrors autoleveling-content.ts. 2026-09-13 history: a `classBuffAbilities` group-heuristic (mirroring classOffensiveAbilities) briefly existed for the buff picker but was REMOVED same day — it kept mis-categorizing real abilities (Shukenja spells leaking cross-class, Faerie Fire, Berserk) faster than the heuristic could be patched, so the buff picker now just shows every ability the selected class actually has (abilitiesForClass, no group filtering) — see CombatStep.tsx and the DSL repo's ability-classification-admin plan, which will eventually replace ALL client-side group-guessing (including classOffensiveAbilities) with a real per-ability admin-curated classification. OFFENSIVE_GROUPS (still live) includes a `SONG_GROUPS` constant (HymnsOfLife/SkaldChants/WarHymns — the server's real GroupType.Songs semantic group names, confirmed against Server.Dsl/ClassAbilityGroups/*.cs) and `'Weather'` (mostly attack spells + Faerie Fire, confirmed via ClassAbilityGroups/Weather.cs).
Gotcha (2026-11-13): the IndexedDB cache keys were bumped to `autolevelingClasses.v2`, `autolevelingAreaIndex.v2` and `autolevelingArea.v2:<slug>` because browsers had cached a stale 31-class list for up to 30 days (the web-server served an offline snapshot with a 30-day Cache-Control). If a catalog shape changes again, bump the suffix; an old key is ignored, never read as a fallback. The wizard shows a notice when the catalog is empty instead of the 5-name fallback silently. Related: pre-fill (autoleveling-class-defaults.ts) reads this catalog, so a class with no abilities in it pre-fills nothing, by design.

/autoleveling-idb.ts
Purpose: Tiny async KvStore abstraction — indexedDbKvStore(db,store) is the browser default; memoryKvStore() is the test/no-IDB drop-in. Backs the area cache and the user-data store.

/autoleveling-content.ts
Purpose: Read-through IndexedDB cache (shatteredarchive-maps DB, 7d soft TTL, network→cache fallback) of the split list/detail API — getAreaIndex() (all areas as lightweight AutoPilotAreaSummary, GET /api/web/maps/autoleveling/areas) + getAreaDetail(slug) (one area's route+targets, cached per slug). Pure helpers areasForLevel / areaBySlug / areasByContinent / isLevelInRange operate on the summary shape; fullRoute needs the detail. __setAreaStoreForTests seam. Mirrors autoleveling-maps-client.ts.

/autoleveling-user-data.ts
Purpose: Player's own auto-leveling additions in a dedicated IndexedDB DB (shatteredarchive-autoleveling/userData): custom targets (per area), buff overlays (per area), fight-command overlays (per area+class), custom paths, prefs (remembered class + player alignment), and a global per-ability learned-cooldown store (2026-09-12: getLearnedCooldown/bumpLearnedCooldown, kind 'abilityTiming', keyed by normalized command string — NOT per-area, lag is a property of the ability). Records carry id + updatedAt for profile cloud-sync (exportAll / importRecords = last-writer-wins) — step 12 wired this pair into useAccountModal.ts's Save/Load-to-cloud via cloudSync.saveAutolevelUserData/loadAutolevelUserData (api/user-content/autoleveling), the same way scripts/plugins/library already sync; see autoleveling-user-data.test.ts's export→JSON→fresh-store→import round-trip test. ensureMigrated() folds in the legacy localStorage stores (autoleveling-saved-targets, autoleveling-user-paths) once. __setUserDataStoreForTests seam. BuffRow = {label, cmd, affect? (if_affect_missing gate), refreshTicks? (tick gate), inCombatCmd? (mid-fight item action), holdNearLevel? (stop refreshing near a ding), unverified? (class-ability guess, UI hint), vitalsGate? (AutoLevelVitalsGate — optional hp/mp/mv %% precondition), onceKey? ('fight'|'round' fire-limit)}; FightRow = {cmd, cooldownSec, vitalsGate?, onceKey?}; AutoLevelPrefs = {playerClass?, playerAlignment?}. 2026-11-13: a 7th kind `targetOverride` = a player's personal edit of a PRE-LOADED (area) target (id `targetOverride:<area>:<key>`, one record per target so two devices write the same record; data {key, lookName?, engageName?, hidden?} holding ONLY what differs from the server value). SYNCED_RECORD_KINDS is a CONTRACT with the server's AutolevelUserRecords.Kinds (DSL repo) and the mobile copy (8 kinds since 2026-11-18: `routeOverride` = a player's whole route for an area, see autoleveling-route-overrides.ts; the server must be deployed BEFORE a client that exports it, or Save to cloud 400s): a kind the server lacks fails the WHOLE Save to cloud with a 400 (abilityTiming did). `normalizeRecord` is the one gate for records arriving from outside (cloud load) and refuses malformed overrides and custom targets whose text has `;`/control characters (it ends up in `kill {name}`). removeCustomTarget/updateCustomTarget exist because the wizard used to drop a removed custom target from the draft only, so it came back on the next seed. Pure rules (key, safe text, diff against default, apply, unmatched) are in autoleveling-target-overrides.ts. FightRow also has `disabled?` (a PAUSED fight command, 2026-11-13): kept with its cooldown/gates/order and synced inside the fight record (the server does not validate a fight record's data), but autoleveling-wizard-config.ts's draftToConfig filters it out of steps.fight.exec; the absent key means active, and resume deletes the key.

/autoleveling-wizard-config.ts
Purpose: draftToConfig(ConfigDraft) → AutoLevelConfig (v3). Maps the wizard draft to what the engine runs: area route (fullRoute) → init.trainingPath (2026-09-12: speedwalkToStart deliberately EXCLUDED — untested bridge, deferred; player starts already at autopilot_start_room); enabled targets → init.targets; buffs → steps.start.pre (if_affect_missing / send_every_ticks / send by gate, carrying vitalsGate?/onceKey? through onto the action); buffs with inCombatCmd|holdNearLevel → config.criticalBuffs[]; fight rows → steps.fight.exec (send_cooldown/send, same vitalsGate?/onceKey? passthrough); adds a `look` to steps.identify; 3s round pause for active leveling (5-min default only for sightsee); draft.restStartOfRound/restEndOfRound (semicolon-separated commands, parseRestCommands) + restDuringRound → config.rest; draft.weightAtOrAbovePct/weightCommands (same parseRestCommands splitter, reused not duplicated) → config.weight (2026-09-13; `weightEnabled` removed same day — no separate on/off flag, a blank commands field IS off). Passes draft.playerAlignment/targetAlignment through when set. ConfigDraft is a local structural type so features/ doesn't import components/ (WizardDraft satisfies it). Buff EXECUTION is the engine's job (autoleveling-engine.ts).

/autoleveling-defaults.ts
Purpose: Default autoleveling config, disabled-by-default. AUTOLEVELING_CONFIG_VERSION = 3 (v2→v3 added criticalBuffs[]). 2026-09-13: added the default `weight` field (90%, no commands — empty commands IS off, no separate enable flag) — backfilled onto old saved configs for free by autoleveling-storage's existing top-level `{...fallback, ...src}` coerce, same as `rest` was.

/autoleveling-engine.ts
Purpose: Runtime that drives a leveling round: rest.startOfRound (wake) → steps.start (buffs) → per-segment move.pre/checkDuringRoundRest/move.post around each gated movement → injected encounters (engage → fight.pre/exec/post → recheckBuffs → postFight → clearQueueIfBuiltUp → identify re-scan) → steps.reset.endRound → rest.endOfRound → steps.reset.wait (or roundLoopTimeMs). Buff runtime: affect-gated (if_affect_missing) + tick (send_every_ticks) buffs are re-evaluated at the top of each round AND after every fight ends (recheckBuffs); "every round" bare sends fire only at round top; tick buffs fire OUT OF COMBAT only. Tracks char_data.tnl drops into a rolling kill-XP estimate — the mob's alignment is read live from the (Golden Aura)/(Red Aura) line prefix (scanMobAlignment; White/Blue/Thorn/etc. are not alignment) and normalized by alignmentXpModifier vs config.playerAlignment; while running writes a bright yellow `[auto-level] +xp · ~N kills to level …` terminal line on each gain AND (2026-09-12) reports the same numbers via deps.setXpProgress for the wizard's "Last kill" row. config.criticalBuffs on game:affect-removed → mid-fight inCombatCmd (item action, anti-flap throttle) only; the recast is recheckBuffs' job. holdNearLevel suppresses recasts once tnl ≤ the estimate. send_cooldown / vitals gates as before. 2026-09-12 additions (combat/rest tuning plan): AutoLevelVitalsGate (vitalsGateSatisfied/vitalsPct) — optional hp/mp/mv %% precondition on send/send_cooldown/send_every_ticks/if_affect_missing, checked before the kind-specific gate, missing-data never blocks; AutoLevelOnceKey 'fight'|'round' (onceGateAllows, recorded only at actual send time so a skipped kind-gate never consumes the slot); round-boundary tracking (trackRoundBoundary, debounced ROUND_GAP_MS=1000 off the shared DAMAGE_LINE_PATTERN, mirrors DslLogViewer's proven heuristic) drives both the round-scoped once-gate AND hasQueueBuildup()/commandsWithQueueBuildup (commands sent vs. rounds observed, fight-wide + per-command) — clearQueueIfBuiltUp() sends `~` only when a queue actually built up, and onAbilityCooldownLearned notifies the hook to persist a +0.5s-bumped cooldownSec per ability (autoleveling-user-data's abilityTiming store) when a fight-command's own per-command buildup fires — emitCooldownLearnedNotice writes a bright terminal "heads up" line alongside it so the change is never silent; AutoLevelRestConfig (rest.startOfRound/endOfRound run at the round's top/reset.endRound point; checkDuringRoundRest/restRuleTriggered/waitForRecovery run an out-of-combat-only threshold→sleep→poll-to-recoverTo→wake cycle, reusing endOfRound/startOfRound as the "how" — status 'resting' is a pre-existing runState variant this is the first real user of). 2026-09-13: shared `writeAutoLevelLine()` (bright-yellow "[auto-level] <body>" terminal line, factored out of emitXpProgress/emitCooldownLearnedNotice) now also fires on run-control transitions — start() ("started (<mode> mode)", or "error: <reason>" for a rejected start via a new `fail()` closure covering all 3 validation guards), stop() ("stopping..."), pause()/resume() ("paused"/"resumed", only on an actual transition — the existing early-return no-ops stay silent), a fatal round-loop error ("error: <message>"), and the final idle transition ("stopped", reached by every exit path — manual stop, natural non-looping completion, or a fatal error). Movement classification now imports classifyMovement/MOVE_DIRS from features/movement/classifyMovement.ts (shared with useCompassBlock.ts) rather than a local isMovementCommand/MOVE_DIRS copy. 2026-09-12 movement-tracking-fix Step 4: movement success/failure is now driven by the shared movementTracker's shatteredarchive:movement-succeeded/-failed events (boundOnMovementResolved), not a direct game:room-data listener that used to treat ANY arrival as success — the tracker does the real {room,sector,exits} diff. A movement-failed whose reasonLine is exactly '(timeout)' (the tracker's own head-of-queue-relative timeout) resolves as an ordinary non-fatal 'failed', same as an explicit server failure line; waitForMovement's own much-larger idleTimeoutMs timer is now a pure backstop (fatal 'timeout', stops the run) reserved for "the tracker never resolved at all." sendCommand no longer dispatches movement-attempt itself (the tracker does, off command-sent). Step 5: waitForEngageOutcome's attempt timeout is now queue-depth-aware — while movementTracker.pendingCount() > 0 at a timeout check, that time is excluded from the attempt's own elapsed budget (polled every 250ms) rather than firing, so a kill command stuck behind a movement burst gets its full attemptTimeout window measured from whenever the shared queue actually drains, not the original send instant; success/not_here resolution is untouched and fires immediately regardless of queue depth. 2026-09-12 flee-pause hardening: waitWhilePausedOrStopped() captures wasPaused and, only on an actual pause→resume transition, calls resyncAfterResume() (sendIdentifyOrLook() + delayMs(lookSettleMs)) before returning — covers every resume (flee-triggered or the manual pause/resume button) for free since it's the one choke point behind all pause-wait call sites; waitForRecovery() now suspends fully (zero action) while paused and excludes paused time from its own timeout budget, so a pause mid-rest-wait never silently "gives up" in the background. 2026-09-13 weight-gate + hot-swap/resync: charVitals gained carryWeight/carryWeightMax (GMCP carry_weight/can_carry_weight); weightPct() is a **0-fallback** (unlike vitalsPct's 100-fallback — absent data must never read as "overweight"); checkWeightGate(round) is out-of-combat only, fetches config fresh per-call (own deps.getConfig(), same pattern as checkDuringRoundRest — this is what makes it hot-swappable), edge-triggered via weightGateFired so the drop sequence fires once per threshold crossing not on every subsequent check; called from the round loop right alongside checkDuringRoundRest. Also this session: `start()`'s local `const cfg` became an instance field `this.cfg` (still fetched ONCE per run, "single consistent cfg" — NOT refactored into a per-round re-fetch) so it CAN be refreshed later; targets-normalization extracted into `refreshTargets(cfg)`; new `refreshRunSnapshot()` (`this.cfg = deps.getConfig(); refreshTargets(this.cfg);`) is called from `waitWhilePausedOrStopped()` at the same point as `resyncAfterResume()` — i.e. only on a real pause→resume transition — so targets/buffs/route/rest-start-end-of-round (which sit on the frozen `this.cfg` all run, unlike fight-commands/rest-during-round/weight which already fetch fresh) pick up a live edit via pause→edit→resume instead of a hot-swap. 2026-09-13 (live-playtest fix): auto-tuned cooldown learning is now cumulative AND live-applied within a run — `learnedCooldownSec` (per-cmd, seeded at `start()` from the persisted `abilityTiming` store via a new `deps.getLearnedCooldown`) is used as a floor under the configured `cooldownSec` for both the `send_cooldown` gate and the next bump's base, so repeated buildups climb (0→0.5→1.0→…) instead of re-deriving the same static-config-based target forever, and a cooldown learned in a past run applies from round 1 of the next one instead of re-earning it from scratch. Also 2026-09-13: `runActions()` now inserts a `lookSettleMs` delay after EACH action specifically when `stepLabel === 'start.pre'` (buffs) — that phase was the one bare-send path with zero pacing (unlike movement's moveSettleMs and fight-commands' auto-tuned cooldowns), so several buffs fired back-to-back with no regard for real cast/violence lag; traced to a live-playtest run where several buffs preceded a hard movement timeout that killed the whole run (`waitForMovement`'s backstop fires if the tracker never resolves — see movement-tracking-fix). Scoped to `start.pre` only, not a new config field — reuses the existing `lookSettleMs` knob. Also 2026-09-13 (live-playtest fix, separate report): rest.startOfRound/endOfRound `send` actions (both the natural per-round hooks AND the during-round low-vitals trigger, which reuses these same two fields — see REST_STEP_LABELS) now route through a new `sendViaCommandProcessor()` → `deps.sendThroughCommandProcessor` (hook wires it to `RuntimeSingleton.Runtime.executeAlias`, same as a manually-typed terminal line) instead of the raw literal `sendCommand()` — a rest sequence configured as the player's own alias/macro ("command stacking") was being sent to the MUD verbatim and doing nothing, since alias expansion never ran. Deliberately scoped to rest only via a `stepLabel` check inside `execAction`'s shared `send` case (same pattern as the buff-settle fix) — movement/fight-commands/buffs/weight stay on the raw path so a user's aliases can never perturb the engine's own timing-sensitive automation. 2026-11-13 (live-log fix): the post-kill re-scan `look` (flushInjected) and a path `look` step no longer wait a fixed lookSettleMs — armRoomScanWait() is armed BEFORE the send and resolves on the reply's "[Exits:" line + 250 ms grace (lookSettleMs kept as a floor). Logs showed replies at 777 ms and 1.5 s against the 500 ms settle: the engine walked on, then the late reply injected an encounter for the room it had left (stray `kill` there, mobs left behind). Same day, second log finding: after a post-fight `cast 'frenzy'` the look was answered 4.5 s later (the cast's wait state holds every later command back), and the first version's 3 s backstop gave up before it. NO REPLY IS ITSELF A LAG SIGNAL: the scan now writes one "probably ability lag" notice at ROOM_SCAN_LAG_NOTICE_MS (3 s) and keeps waiting up to ROOM_SCAN_HARD_CAP_MS (20 s, then says it gave up); a reply with no Exits line (pitch black, blind) also counts as the answer (ROOM_REPLY_RE). Not done: buff casts in start.pre / recheckBuffs are still paced by a fixed lookSettleMs, not by their own replies.

/autoleveling-index.ts
Purpose: Centralized export file for autoleveling feature with backward compatibility.

/autoleveling-mudlet-import.ts
Purpose: Imports and converts Mudlet leveling script data into buildable path steps.

/autoleveling-normalize.ts
Purpose: Config repair + migration. `normalizeAutoLevelConfig` (currently unused) repairs old/broken shapes + old `enabled:bool`→`mode`. `migrateAutoLevelConfigV2ToV3` (used by autoleveling-storage) carries a v2 blob forward: same shape + version:3 + criticalBuffs:[].

/autoleveling-saved-targets.ts
Purpose: Stores and manages manually entered auto-leveling targets per game zone in local storage.

/autoleveling-storage.ts
Purpose: Per-connection config persistence in localStorage. Key `autoleveling-config-v3:<connId>`; hard-gates version 3. On a v3 miss it reads the old `autoleveling-config-v2:` key, migrates forward (migrateAutoLevelConfigV2ToV3), and re-saves under v3. coerceConfig fills missing fields from the default.

/autoleveling-text.ts
Purpose: Processes and normalizes text input for autoleveling functionality.

/autoleveling-types.ts
Purpose: Persisted config schema (v3) + runtime runState types. AutoLevelConfig carries criticalBuffs[], playerAlignment/targetAlignment (AutoLevelAlignment, absent = neutral), the step triplets, a required `rest` (AutoLevelRestConfig, 2026-09-12) and `weight` (AutoLevelWeightConfig, 2026-09-13 — {atOrAbovePct, commands: AutoLevelAction[]}, ONE rule not an array like Rest's duringRound; `commands` is parsed AutoLevelAction[] in the final config, same as Rest's start/end-of-round — the raw semicolon-string only lives in the wizard draft; no `enabled` flag — that was dropped same day per live-playtest feedback, a blank `commands` list IS off), and timing knobs. AutoLevelStepConfig no longer has the dead `trainingPath` field — the route is init.trainingPath. Actions incl. send_every_ticks, plus (2026-09-12) optional vitalsGate (AutoLevelVitalsGate: stat hp|mp|mv, op above|below, pct) and onceKey (AutoLevelOnceKey: 'fight'|'round') on send/send_cooldown/send_every_ticks/if_affect_missing — both are engine-composable preconditions, distinct from the older single-purpose if_hp/mp/mv_pct_below kinds which predate the wizard and stay as-is. AutoLevelRestConfig = {startOfRound, endOfRound: AutoLevelAction[], duringRound: AutoLevelRestDuringRoundRule[]}; a rule ({hp?,mp?,mv?,recoverTo}) requires ALL present thresholds to trigger. AutoLevelXpProgress is a separate piece of state (not on AutoLevelRunState) so it survives status transitions. Alignment math is in autoleveling-alignment.ts.

/autopilot-signals.ts
Purpose: Pure transition detector behind useAutopilotSignals: status changes are reported once, need-help only when ENTERING error (a repeat error while still in error is silent; leaving and re-entering emits again), and idle is the assumed start so an idle run is silent. Nothing here knows who listens.

/autoleveling-target-overrides.ts
Purpose: Pure rules for a player's personal edits of the server's pre-loaded area targets (added 2026-11-13). The key is the normalized ORIGINAL room line (lowercase, collapsed whitespace); only differences from the server value are stored, so a later server fix still reaches untouched fields; an override whose key left the served list is kept and reported `unmatched`, never dropped or applied (a rename must not erase an edit; it re-attaches if the key returns). Text is refused if empty, over 200 chars, or containing `;`, line breaks or control characters (E3: it is sent as `kill {name}`; the server validator enforces the same). Storage and sync are in autoleveling-user-data.ts; tests in autoleveling-target-overrides.test.ts.

/autoleveling-class-defaults.ts
Purpose: Pure pre-fill rules for a class's buffs and fight commands (added 2026-11-13). Buffs: the DEFAULT_BUFF_LABELS entries (protection and damage; not detections, travel, alignment protections) that the class really has at the character's level, built from the log-verified BUFF_CATALOG (exact command + GMCP affect) via buffRowFromCatalog, which CombatStep's manual catalog add shares. Fight: up to DEFAULT_FIGHT_ROWS (3) from the class's offensive abilities, the strongest spells/songs first (two, so a hybrid keeps room), then known combat skills in COMBAT_SKILL_NAMES order. Known limit: ~25% of catalog abilities have no semantic group, so skill picks lean on COMBAT_SKILL_NAMES; the player edits. Only used when nothing was saved (see CombatStep).

/autoleveling-route-overrides.ts
Purpose: Pure rules for a player's own route of an area (added 2026-11-18). Unlike a target override it stores the WHOLE `dirs` list (one `routeOverride` record per area, id `routeOverride:<slug>`), because a list has no useful per-field diff; a route equal to the served one leaves no record. `requiredActions` are never part of it (fullRoute still puts the server's first). Each step is sent to the game, so a step is refused if empty, over 200 chars, or has `;`/line break/control character (same isSafeTargetText as targets); max 1000 steps. The C# validator (AutolevelUserRecords.TryValidateRouteOverride, DSL repo) enforces the same: change both together. The editor splits text on `;` and line breaks (parseRouteText). A later server fix to the route does NOT reach a player with an override; they see "Shared route: N steps" and can reset. Storage in autoleveling-user-data.ts (getRouteOverride/setRouteOverride/resetRouteOverride); tests in autoleveling-route-overrides.test.ts and AutoLevelingWizard.route.test.tsx.

/autoleveling-command-text.ts
Purpose: the ONE place a stored command is filled in before it goes to the game (CMDTPL-01). `expandCommand` fills {name}/{target}/{keyword} with the engaged keyword and, with no target, drops the placeholder and its space; `findUnresolvedPlaceholder` finds a leftover {word} or <words> (a seeded `quaff <potion>`); `isItemAction` is quaff/brandish/zap/recite/eat/drink. Relation: the engine's `outgoingText` is the only caller on the send path, so every send (fight, buff, in-combat, rest, identify, initiation) goes through it; never add a send that skips it. Why: on 2026-11-14 the game received `cast 'fog' {name}` 30 times (and `{n` is a colour code, so the echo showed an orange `ame}`) and the seeded `quaff <potion>` 23 times. Tests: autoleveling-command-text.test.ts and the `commands reach the game as real commands` block of autoleveling-engine.test.ts.

Row options (2026-11-18): BuffRow and FightRow may carry combatState ('in'|'out', absent = either), enemyGate ({op, band} with band one of ENEMY_BAND_FLOORS) and, buffs only, retryUntilSet (only ever stored as false; absent = on for an affect row). normalizeRecord checks them on the way in from the cloud with normalizeRowOptions: an unknown value is dropped from the ROW, never the row. They ride in the existing buff and fight records (no server change: the server does not look inside their data). draftToConfig carries them to the actions (extraGates) and critical buffs. Behaviour lands in the engine in later phases; tests in autoleveling-row-options.test.ts.

/autoleveling-enemy-condition.ts
Purpose: reads the enemy's condition PHRASE ("has quite a few wounds") out of game text, because GMCP has no enemy health. CONDITION_BANDS are the stock base's edges (100/90/75/50/30/15/0; "bleeding to death" shares 0): DSL's are NOT verified, so every label says "about N%". Relation: the engine's trackEnemyCondition keeps one band for the creature being fought only (the subject must be the start of the matched room line, as boundOnCreatureDeath tests the death line) and clears it at fight end and at the next fight; enemyGateSatisfied never blocks while it is null. ANSI is stripped before matching because the game colours text mid-phrase. Tests: autoleveling-enemy-condition.test.ts and autoleveling-engine.gates.test.ts.

/autoleveling-cast-outcome.ts
Purpose: the game's own words for how a cast went, ONLY lines seen in the 2026-11-14 and 2026-11-18 logs ("You lost your concentration." retry; "You can't concentrate enough." no mana; "You aren't fighting anyone."; "You do not have that potion." item gone) plus the catalog success line. Relation: the engine's armCastWatch/observeCastText read it only inside the 3 s window after the engine's own send, so a failure line from a cast the player typed is never attributed to the engine. Rule (RUNLOOP-03): every re-arm command goes through castUntilSet (one in flight, capped retry) and every event-driven one through enqueueRearm; never add a direct sendCommand for a re-arm. Tests: autoleveling-cast-outcome.test.ts, autoleveling-engine.rearm.test.ts.

/autoleveling-lag.ts
Purpose: how long the game will still be busy with commands the engine itself sent (lagAllowanceMs models them queued one behind the other, capped 20 s) and effectiveLagMs (largest of configured, learned, server lag; lag is a floor). Rule (RUNLOOP-01): an outer fatal timer must be longer than the inner timeout it backs up PLUS this allowance: the movement backstop is max(idleTimeoutMs, 4 x HEAD_TIMEOUT_MS) + lagAllowanceMs, because it used to be idleTimeoutMs = 1000 ms, shorter than the tracker's own 1200 ms, and ended runs. Expiry calls pauseForTimeout (status paused, reason timeout, Copilot is told); only Stop and real errors end a run. Tests: autoleveling-engine.pause.test.ts, autoleveling-lag.test.ts.

/autoleveling-step-prefs.ts
Purpose: the pure rules for what the Rest and Weight steps and the lap wait remember (stored inside the global pref:global record, only what differs from the default) and for the per-area unticked-target list (record pref:targets:<areaSlug>). normalizeStepPrefs / normalizeDeselected check everything coming from storage or the cloud and drop what is invalid without dropping the record. Relation: both ride on kind pref, which the server (AutolevelUserRecords.cs) accepts with any data, so there is NO server change; a new top-level field here needs only this module and getPrefs/saveStepValues in autoleveling-user-data.ts. The wizard writes them debounced and flushes on close (AutoLevelingWizard.tsx); seeding an area never writes (selectionSeed). Tests: autoleveling-step-prefs.test.ts, AutoLevelingWizard.remembered.test.tsx.

Class feed timing (2026-11-19): AutoPilotAbility may carry durationTicks and lagMs; the server OMITS them when unknown and 0 is real, so normalizeAbility (autoleveling-content-types.ts) keeps a key only for a whole number from 0 up to a sane maximum, and the extract script (scripts/extract-autoleveling-classes.mjs) does the same: both used to drop every field they did not know, which would have hidden the data. The catalog cache key is autolevelingClasses.v3 for the same reason. Tests: autoleveling-ability-timing.test.ts; the web-server snapshot test is apps/web-server/src/mapsResponsePolicy.test.ts.

Server timing as defaults (2026-11-19): autoleveling-lag.ts also holds the timing index (buildTimingIndex from the class catalog, abilityNameFromCommand, serverLagMsFor) and the two default rules (refreshTicksFromDuration, cooldownSecFromLag). The engine only ever USES the server lag as a FLOOR (effectiveLagMs = largest of configured, learned, server; unknown ignored; a measured 0 is no floor): send_cooldown, retryGapMs and the lag a send is remembered with. New rows (class-defaults, CombatStep add-ability) are the only place a default is written; saved rows are never touched. hooks/useAbilityTimingIndex.ts loads the index from the cached catalog (kept apart from useAutoLeveling.ts, which jest cannot compile: it uses import.meta). Tests: autoleveling-lag.test.ts, autoleveling-timing-defaults.test.ts, autoleveling-engine.lag.test.ts, hooks/useAbilityTimingIndex.test.tsx.

/autoleveling-step-waits.ts
Purpose: Rules for the route steps the game takes seconds to finish, and for the player's own slow commands. Why (game-server logs 2026-11-19..25): a `heel` is answered 0.1-10.9 s later ("comes running in") and a `ride st` 0.05-12.4 s later ("You mount"); the engine used to wait a fixed lookSettleMs after any non-move step, so 16 of 99 mounts had a move sent before the mount landed. Only lines seen in those logs are listed; every other step keeps the fixed settle. `onMiss`: a failed or unanswered `ride` PAUSES the run (walking on unmounted sends the character into fights without its charge), a `heel` only notes it (the ride after it has its own check). `manualCommandKind` is what the engine treats as the player's own slow command (`c`/`cast` + argument, heel, ride); `whoc`, `imp`, looks, kills never hold it. Used by the engine's armStepWatch/finishSlowStep (route steps), noteWireCommand/yieldToManual (player's commands). Tests: autoleveling-step-waits.test.ts and autoleveling-engine.steps.test.ts.
Related, same engine file: isRedundantBuffRecast skips, in recheckBuffs only, a PLAIN send of a catalog buff whose affect is up (cast 'shield' row with a gate was cast after every fight, 61 times with 54 ticks left); tick rows are left alone because refreshing early is their job. The engine's own sends are remembered (ownSends) so the shatteredarchive:command-sent signal (every wire command, from any source) can tell the player typing from the engine; a send through the command processor writes a `*` entry for 1.5 s because an alias may expand into anything.
